eeded.\n\nWe have our own notes. We can either support Candidate B or propose another candidate. We need to consider criteria and evidence. Let's examine all candidates:\n\nCandidate A: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities. Concerns: regarded as a show\u2011off, not open to new ideas. Also regarded as a show\u2011off (maybe arrogance). Not open to new ideas = low adaptability. Leadership good, but show\u2011off may affect crew cooperation. No explicit stress resilience info. Technical competence maybe high (spatial vision, complex connections). But concerns about cooperation and adaptability.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress resilience), good at assessing weather (technical), excellent computer skills (technical). Concerns: makes nasty remarks about colleagues (uncooperative), grumpy. Also weak memory for numbers (attention/accuracy). So concerns about crew cooperation and attention to numeric data.\n\nCandidate C: can make correct decisions quickly (decision quality), keeps calm in crisis (stress resilience), understands complicated technology (technical), but difficulty communicating ideas (crew cooperation), regarded as egocentric (cooperation), not willing to further education (adaptability). So strengths in decision speed, stress, technical, but cooperation and adaptability concerns.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress resilience?), can concentrate very well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical/problem solving), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility), but regarded as know\u2011it\u2011all (cooperation), hot temper (stress resilience, crew cooperation). So strengths in problem solving, responsibility, concentration, but concerns about temperament and cooperation.\n\nWe need to discuss and maybe bring up another candidate. Since Agent 1 recommended B, we could either support B, or argue that another candidate is better overall. We should bring in evidence about other candidates to compare.\n\nWe could argue that Candidate C's calmness in crisis and quick correct decisions outweigh communication issues, but communication is critical for crew. Or argue Candidate D's responsibility and problem solving are strong, but hot temper is serious.\n\nMaybe we can propose Candidate C as a better fit because decision quality and calmness are crucial, and we can mitigate communication issues with training. But the egocentric and unwillingness to further education are concerns.\n\nAlternatively, we could support B but highlight the numeric memory weakness and ask if that is a serious issue for long-distance flights (e.g., fuel calculations). Could ask Agent 1 for more detail on B's numeric memory impact.\n\nWe can also ask Agent 3 for their view on Candidate D's hot temper.\n\nGiven the process, we should make a focused argumentative contribution, referencing Agent 1's recommendation, using facts, and possibly adjusting our vote.\n\nWe need to decide our vote. We could either stay neutral, but we must state a vote. The structured notes show we haven't voted yet. We can either vote for B, C, D, or A.\n\nWhich candidate seems strongest overall? Let's weigh:\n\n- B: strong reliability, stress, technical, but crew cooperation issue and numeric memory. Crew cooperation is essential; nasty remarks could be dangerous. Numeric memory weakness could affect calculations.\n\n- C: strong decision speed, calm, technical, but communication issues, egocentric, not willing to further education. Communication is also essential; egocentric may affect teamwork.\n\n- D: strong problem solving, responsibility, concentration, but hot temper and know-it-all attitude. Temper could affect stress resilience and crew cooperation.\n\n- A: strong anticipation, complex connections, spatial vision, leadership, but show-off and not open to new ideas (cooperation/adaptability). No explicit stress resilience.\n\nAll have cooperation concerns. Which is least severe? B's nasty remarks are direct hostility. C's difficulty communicating ideas and egocentric may be less overt hostility but still problematic. D's hot temper could be severe. A's show-off may be less severe but not open to new ideas is adaptability issue.\n\nReliability: B is very conscientious, strong. Others not mentioned.\n\nStress resilience: B handles stress well, C keeps calm, D responds adequately but hot temper, A unknown.\n\nTechnical competence: A (spatial vision, complex connections), B (weather, computer), C (technology), D (problem solving).\n\nDecision quality: C quick correct decisions, D solves problems extremely well, A anticipates dangerous situations, B not mentioned.\n\nAttention/accuracy: D concentrates well, B weak memory for numbers, A not mentioned, C not mentioned.\n\nCrew cooperation: B nasty remarks, C difficulty communicating, egocentric, D know-it-all, hot temper, A show-off, not open to ideas.\n\nResponsibility: D takes responsibility seriously, A leadership, B conscientious, C not mentioned.\n\nAdaptability: A not open to new ideas, B maybe okay, C not willing to further education, D hot temper, know-it-all.\n\nOverall, B seems strongest on reliability and stress, but crew cooperation is a serious red flag. C also strong on decision and calm, but communication issues.\n\nGiven safety-critical, crew cooperation is essential. Nasty remarks could be fatal. Might be disqualifying. So perhaps we should shift to Candidate C as the best overall, assuming we can mitigate communication issues.\n\nWe need to bring evidence: C keeps calm in crisis, can make correct decisions quickly, understands complicated technology. These address stress resilience, decision quality, technical competence. Concerns: difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education. We can argue that while cooperation concerns exist, they may be less severe than overt hostility, and can be addressed with training.\n\nThus we can propose Candidate C as the current recommendation.\n\nWe should reference Agent 1's points about B, and then present our case for C, comparing strengths and concerns.\n\nWe can also ask Agent 1 for more detail on B's nasty remarks severity, but maybe not needed.\n\nWe need to produce public message with our vote for Candidate C.\n\nLet's craft: \"I appreciate Anna's focus on B, but I have concerns about the crew cooperation issue. In contrast, Candidate C demonstrates calmness in crisis, quick correct decisions, and strong technical understanding, which are critical. While C has communication difficulties and appears egocentric, these may be mitigated. Therefore I currently recommend Candidate C.\"\n\nMake sure to reference explicit facts: \"keeps calm in a crisis\", \"can make correct decisions quickly\", \"understands complicated technology\". Also mention concerns: \"difficulty communicating ideas\", \"regarded as egocentric\", \"not very willing to further his education\". Then explain implication.\n\nNow produce metadata JSON with vote \"Candidate C\"."}
